  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t)\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\sqrt{t}+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(t)+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(4)=\u27e85,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4)+2\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =\u27e8\\sqrt{4}+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4)+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e85,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4)+2\u27e9 & =\u27e82+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4)+{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4)+2 & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4)+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=\u27e8\\sqrt{t}+3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(t)+2\u27e9.\n$$",
